  "text_md": "**Table 4. Clinical Trials of Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis**\n\n## Trial Study Population Location Intervention Outcome Comments\n\n**TDF2** 1,219 sexually active Botswana Daily oral TDF/ 63% protection >30% did not complete\n\n|adults; 55% male, 45%||FTC||study; cannot draw|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|female; 94% unmarried;||||definitive conclusions|\n|approximately 90% aged||||for women and men|\n|21–29||||separately.|\n|4,758 serodiscordant|Botswana, Kenya,|Daily oral TDF or|67% protection with TDF|Serodiscordant couples|\n|heterosexual couples; 38%|Rwanda, South|TDF/FTC|alone; 75% protection with|may be a distinct,|\n|HIV-negative female, 68%|Africa, Tanzania,||TDF/FTC|unique population.|\n|HIV-negative male partner;|Uganda, Zambia||||\n\n**PIP**\n\n98% married; median age 33\n\n## FEM-PrEP\n\n|1,951 heterosexual women|Kenya, South|Daily oral TDF/|Trial discontinued for|Adherence assessment|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|aged 18–35 at high risk of infection|Africa, Tanzania|FTC|futility in April 2011.|with monthly clinical samples to measure|\n\ndrug concentration is pending.\n\n**VOICE** 5,029 heterosexual women Uganda, South Daily oral TDF or No study drug significantly Adherence to study MTN-003 aged 18–45 in high-Africa, Zimbabwe daily oral TDF/ reduced the risk of HIV drugs was low; TFV prevalence areas FTC or daily acquisition. Estimates of was detected in 30% of topical TFV gel effectiveness were less the oral TDF arm, 29% than 0 for TDF and TDF/ in the oral TDF/FTC FTC daily oral dosing arm, and 25% in the (negative 48.8% and TDF gel arm. negative 4.2% TDF/FTC respectively), and reduced risk of HIV infection of\n\n14.7% for TDF gel.\n**Key to Acronyms:** FTC = emtricitabine; TDF = tenofovir disoproxil fumarate; TFV = tenofovir\n\nSource: Adapted from: Kashuba et al., Pre-exposure prophylaxis for HIV prevention: how to predict success: Table Antiretroviral-based HIV\n\nprevention studies. *Lancet*. 2012;379(9835): 2409-2411.\n\n*Recommendations for the Use of Antiretroviral Drugs in Pregnant Women with HIV Infection and Interventions* *to Reduce Perinatal HIV Transmission in the United States*\n\n# Downloaded from [https://aidsinfo.nih.gov/guidelines](https://aidsinfo.nih.gov/guidelines) on 8/22/2018\n",
